    {
      "title": "Ukraine’s Zelenskyy praises US over $2.1bn Patriot missile deal but warns it could take years",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/31/ukraines-zelenskyy-praises-us-over-21bn-patriot-missile-deal-but-warns-it-could-take-years",
      "description": "<p>The Ukrainian president called the US decision strong, but added that delivery of 500 Patriot PAC-3 missiles ⁠could take years</p><p>The US has approved the sale ⁠of 500 Patriot PAC-3 missiles ⁠to Ukraine for an estimated $2.1bn, but Volodymyr Zelenskyy has warned deliveries could take years and stockpiles were needed now to counter Russian ballistic missile attacks.</p><p>“The proposed sale will improve Ukraine’s capability to meet current and future threats by improving its integrated air and missile defense capabilities,” the US state department said in a statement on Friday.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/31/ukraines-zelenskyy-praises-us-over-21bn-patriot-missile-deal-but-warns-it-could-take-years\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-31T02:18:17"
    },
    {
      "title": "US approves $2.1 billion Patriot missile sale to Ukraine, funded by allies",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/us-approves-2-1-billion-patriot-missile-sale-to-ukraine-funded-by-allies-126103100055_1.html",
      "description": "The Trump administration has said that it has approved a proposed USD 2.1 billion sale of Patriot air defense interceptors for Ukraine after Kyiv had accused the Pentagon of delaying delivery of the vital munition needed to stop Russian ballistic missiles.\nThe State Department said 500 Patriot missiles are being purchased for Ukraine with donations from Canada, Germany and Norway through a US programme and that no American funds were involved.\n\"This proposed sale will support the foreign policy and national security objectives of the United States by improving the security of a partner country that is a force for political and economic stability in Europe,\" the department said in a press release.\nUkrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy has warned this month that his country had nearly run out of the vital military munition it needs to combat Moscow's attacks as tensions with US President Donald Trump's administration escalated.\nThe Ukrainian leader had accused the Pentagon of delay",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-31T02:45:06"
    },
    {
      "title": "US greenlights $2.1bn sale of Patriot interceptors to Ukraine",
